The RFCG ATP Reduction Initiative was created to reduce the number of tests that are performed to decrease the amount of time needed to test a module while also not affecting the production yield of the module.  Effort was also used to reach out to Systems Engineering for various modules   Two different methodologies were used to determine which tests were redundant to avoid the possibility of removing a test that would pass in scenario but could fail in another. 

The first method was a “Go/No-Go” method which activates immediately upon a single module in the series failing a test.  Tests that are marked as “Go” are deemed fundamentally necessary to demonstrate the device has been properly manufactured and provides the assumption that the device is fully operational and meets the required specifications.  Tests marked as “No-Go” are ones that are carried out but are not fundamentally necessary to demonstrate the device’s functionality as they were proven during the DVT.  These tests can also be ones that were found to be statistically redundant. 

The second method was to use the test data from each module to show how the tests were redundant.  The mean and three standard deviations were calculated to show that over 99% of the units assembled would pass the test.  The upper limit of this redundancy check was set to be the default of the median of the mean of the units tested and the test specification as it would avoid being too strict (a lower median) or too lax (a higher median). 

The graph shown in Figure 2.1.1 is for a spur test that is not redundant while the graph in Figure 2.1.2 is for a spur test that is redundant.  A purple line is used as the redundancy check limit which is user-adjustable and can be increased or decreased.  The light green line represents the 3rd standard deviation from the mean of the data taken while the red line is the designed-to specification. 

As noted with the graph in Figure 2.1.1, the data is spread over a wider margin.  Some units were above the “half-way” median marker, resulting in the test being deemed “not redundant”.   In the scenario of the graph on the right, the data is closely packed together with little variation resulting in the test being marked as redundant. 


The first version of the MATLAB script was created to parse the CUI data for each module and simultaneously compute the statistics above and provide an estimate to the total time saved.  The time saved per unit and a dollar per hour estimate was extrapolated to 1000 units to provide a total savings value.  The data obtained were downloaded from the CITED/METABASE web applications while the block time durations were obtained from units that had passed all of the tests without any skips.  Tests were then marked as “Redundant” if every single unit had passed over a one year span, “Not Redundant” if a unit had gotten too close to the failure boundary, or “Not Redundant (failed limit)” if it had failed to meet the specification.  Tests that had less than 32 points were ignored due to them not meeting the minimum sample size of the Central Limit Theorem.  A longer duration test was also preferred as it would have a greater ROI than a shorter duration test. 

This MATLAB script was later developed into a more advanced version that relies less on the basic limit statistics. The new MATLAB script 




